# Sprint 4 — Student Portal

## Overview

Sprint 4 gives students their front door: viewing what teachers have
posted, and submitting homework through the same marking engine
teachers use. No marking logic is duplicated — homework submission
reuses Sprint 3's `submissions.py` path directly, same pipeline as
teacher-side marking.

## Goals

- Let a student view study materials and assignments
- Let a student submit homework for an assignment and see the marked
  result, using the existing engine
- Guarantee a student can never submit homework attributed to another
  student, even by a tampered request
- Let a student view their own marked results, and only their own
- Carry forward Sprint 3's interface and access-control standards
  rather than establishing new ones

## What was built

- **Study materials view** — list and detail view of `StudyMaterial`
  items, visible to all students for now (no class scoping yet — same
  known limitation carried from Sprint 3)
- **Assignments view** — list view of `Assignment` items (title,
  instructions, due date), each showing whether the logged-in student
  has already submitted homework for it
- **`Paper.assignment`** — new nullable FK linking a submission back
  to the assignment it answers, distinct from its memorandum
- **Homework submission** — student selects an assignment, uploads a
  photo, submits through Sprint 3's existing marking pipeline: same
  compression, same AI call, same defensive parsing
- **Server-set student ownership** — `Paper.student` is set from
  `request.user` server-side, never from any client-supplied field.
  Covered by an explicit test that attempts to submit with a spoofed
  student ID and confirms it's rejected/ignored.
- **Graceful failure state** — reuses Sprint 3's clear retry pattern
  on a failed marking attempt, no crash, no duplicated UI logic
- **Own results view** — a student can see their own marked papers
  (score, per-question feedback, most recent first); the same
  ownership boundary from Sprint 3 prevents viewing another student's
  results, enforced at the view level
- **Access control** — all views reject non-student roles, consistent
  with Sprints 1 and 3
- **Tests** — role rejection, the student-spoofing attempt, the
  mocked marking flow, and assignment-submission-status display

## Definition of done

- [x] Student can view study materials and assignments
- [x] Student can submit homework for an assignment and see the
      marked result
- [x] `Paper.student` is proven server-set, not client-controlled —
      covered by an explicit spoofing test
- [x] A failed marking attempt shows the same clear retry state as
      the teacher flow
- [x] Student can view their own results, and cannot access another
      student's results by URL-guessing
- [x] All views reject non-student roles at the view level
- [x] Tests cover role rejection, the spoofing attempt, the mocked
      marking flow, and submission-status display

## Interface standards carried forward

18px body text, 48px minimum tap targets, AA contrast, visible focus,
works without JavaScript — the bar Sprint 3 established, not a
one-off for that sprint alone.

## Out of scope for this sprint

Teacher visibility into homework results (Sprint 7's dashboard),
facial recognition attendance (Sprint 5), parent notifications
(Sprint 6), photo-to-transcribe (a teacher-authoring feature from
Sprint 3, not relevant to students).

## Known limitations

- **No class/roster structure yet** (carried over from Sprint 3) —
  study materials and assignments are visible to all students, not
  scoped to a class.
- **Parent-student linking is still self-declared and unverified**
  (carried over from Sprint 1) — unchanged by this sprint.

## Next up — Sprint 5

Build facial recognition attendance: a teacher-triggered single scan
per student, once at morning arrival and once at afternoon departure.